{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W7VJkjewE4QBqRSU3NGDUJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose tasks for release 02.0.\n\nRelease 02.0 is \"Batch 2 — trivial utilities (true, false, yes, basename, dirname)\" with 5 use cases, all at spec_complete status.\n\nI can only propose 1 task. Let me focus on the earliest/simplest one.\n\nThe use cases are:\n- rel02.0-uc001-true\n- rel02.0-uc002-false\n- rel02.0-uc003-yes\n- rel02.0-uc004-basename\n- rel02.0-uc005-dirname\n\n\nI need to locate the PRDs for these utilities. Looking at the existing PRD numbering pattern, I can see there's coverage for testutils, sys, format, ls, and du, but I need to identify which PRD handles the trivial utilities like true, false, yes, basename, and dirname. The true and false commands are straightforward—true exits with 0, false exits with 1—and might be implementable as a single task. I'm realizing I need to anchor to specific PRD IDs, but I can't access the actual PRD files due to the constraints. Given the project's naming convention and the fact that true and false are trivial implementations, I should propose combining them into a single task and reference the likely PRD IDs based on the rel02.0 release structure—probably something like prd012 for these basic utilities. I need to figure out the PRD file paths to reference in required_reading. Since I can't access the filesystem directly, I'll use the naming convention the stitch agent can follow to locate them. For combining true and false into a single implementation task, I should reference whichever PRDs cover both utilities, keeping in mind they're each just a few lines of code. I'm realizing the test suite for rel02.0 would be located at docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-02.0.yaml, and since true and false are so minimal, they probably each have just a handful of requirements. Let me draft a combined task for implementing both commands together, keeping the PRD references reasonable so the stitch agent can locate them. I'm not certain about the exact PRD file numbers, so I'll note that in the task and let the stitch agent locate the correct files based on the naming pattern. Since these utilities are standalone and don't require additional dependencies beyond what's needed for testing, I can make the task self-contained with enough context for the agent to work with.\n\nFor the implementations themselves, true and false are straightforward—each just needs to handle argument parsing for --help and --version flags, then exit with the appropriate code (0 for true, 1 for false), ignoring any other arguments passed in. I'll combine both into a single implementation task and reference the relevant PRDs and use cases as required reading. use-cases/rel02.0-uc002-false.yaml\n\nThe test suite:\n- docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-02.0.yaml\n\nFor the PRDs, I'll use a glob pattern in required_reading since I can't explore the filesystem directly—something like \"docs/specs/product-requirements/prd*-true.yaml\" should capture them.\n\nNow I'm realizing the constraints are strict: exactly 1 task maximum, and each task can have at most 4 PRD sub-requirements. Since the true and false implementations are trivial, I'll combine them into a single task—they'll easily stay under 150 lines combined. Let me finalize the proposal now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27514,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27514},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30b09f9a-912b-4bea-99d2-5a22e2af5cc0","uuid":"90ccb59f-70e8-4caf-bd4c-8ad8ceb22273"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W7VJkjewE4QBqRSU3NGDUJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils stress-tests spec-driven code generation by reimplementing GNU utilities in Go, verifying parity via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Shared libraries in pkg/ (sys, format, testutils), per-utility packages in cmd/, mage-driven build/orchestration. Specs are human-authored; code is AI-generated.\n\n**Current state:** 10 releases with specs exist. Releases 00.0, 01.1–01.3, 03.0, 04.0–04.1 are \"implemented\" (specs + code at generation tags). Releases 02.0–02.2, 05.0–05.4, 99.2 are \"spec_complete\" (PRDs, use cases, test suites written; no code yet).\n\n**Current release:** rel02.0 — \"Batch 2: trivial utilities (true, false, yes, basename, dirname).\" Five use cases, all spec_complete, no code generated yet.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nrel02.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Its five utilities (true, false, yes, basename, dirname) are trivial. `true` and `false` are the simplest possible utilities (~10 lines each) with no pkg/ dependencies. They share an identical structure (exit with a fixed code), so combining them into one task follows P2 (\"combine trivial changes\"). This task has no dependencies on other rel02.0 work and should be first per P8 (alphabetical file path tiebreaker: cmd/false before cmd/true, but they're combined).\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/true and cmd/false -- prd true R1-R2, prd false R1-R2\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section -- differential testing pattern, SIGPIPE handling, graceful skip)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find the PRDs for true and false utilities)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel02.0-uc001-true.yaml (true use case)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel02.0-uc002-false.yaml (false use case)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-02.0.yaml (test suite for rel02.0)\n      - cmd/cat/main.go (reference for cmd/ package structure and SIGPIPE pattern)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference for differential test structure)\n      - pkg/testutils/ (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests API)\n      - pkg/sys/ (InstallSIGPIPEHandler API)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/true/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"GNU true implementation: exit 0, support --help and --version\"\n      - path: cmd/true/true_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests against gtrue reference binary\n      - path: cmd/false/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"GNU false implementation: exit 1, support --help and --version\"\n      - path: cmd/false/false_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests against gfalse reference binary\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement cmd/true/main.go. The binary accepts any arguments and\n          ignores them, exiting with status 0. Support --help (print usage to\n          stdout, exit 0) and --version (print version to stdout, exit 0).\n          Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per the\n          project SIGPIPE protocol.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement cmd/false/main.go. The binary accepts any arguments and\n          ignores them, exiting with status 1. Support --help (print usage to\n          stdout, exit 0) and --version (print version to stdout, exit 0).\n          Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per the\n          project SIGPIPE protocol.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement differential tests in cmd/true/true_test.go using\n          pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests. Build the Go binary with\n          testutils.BuildBinary. Look up the reference binary \"gtrue\" via\n          exec.LookPath; t.Skip if not found. Test cases: no args (exit 0),\n          arbitrary args ignored (exit 0), --help (exit 0, stdout non-empty),\n          --version (exit 0, stdout non-empty).\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement differential tests in cmd/false/false_test.go using\n          pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests. Build the Go binary with\n          testutils.BuildBinary. Look up the reference binary \"gfalse\" via\n          exec.LookPath; t.Skip if not found. Test cases: no args (exit 1),\n          arbitrary args ignored (exit 1), --help (exit 0, stdout non-empty),\n          --version (exit 0, stdout non-empty).\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Follow the existing cmd/ package structure visible in cmd/cat/:\n          main.go for implementation, <utility>_test.go for differential\n          tests. Each package compiles to a standalone binary.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Use the graceful skip pattern from ARCHITECTURE shared_protocols:\n          call exec.LookPath for the reference binary at the start of\n          TestDiff, t.Skip if not found. Never install the reference binary\n          in test code.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          Set LC_ALL=C in differential test environment per project convention.\n          Not strictly necessary for true/false (no output comparison beyond\n          exit code), but maintains consistency with other cmd/ test files.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/true/main.go compiles and exits 0 when run with no arguments\n      - id: AC2\n        text: cmd/false/main.go compiles and exits 1 when run with no arguments\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/true/ passes (or skips gracefully if gtrue not in PATH)\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/false/ passes (or skips gracefully if gfalse not in PATH)\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: Both binaries handle --help and --version flags matching GNU behavior\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/true/ ./cmd/false/ reports no issues\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27514,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27514},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30b09f9a-912b-4bea-99d2-5a22e2af5cc0","uuid":"300f6ea9-d296-4dae-930c-4f7d43c34e2a"}
